 - **Host inventory** (#1905) — `lib/host-inventory.js`. The one answer to "what name does this machine serve on its overlay network". It holds one `normalizeHostName`, one overlay-DNS probe with a provider registry (Tailscale's `Self.DNSName` is the only entry), a process-wide observation that certificate generation refreshes, and `resolveTailnetHost`: the configured `caddyTailnetHost`, else the detected name, with drift and omission reported. `certHostUnion` (and through it the cert names and the served-Host allowlist) and the operator-link fallback all read it, so a link never names a host the server refuses. A detected name is never written into the Caddy site, because that site needs a gate. `POST /api/setup/generate-cert` reports the inventory. It always unions the mandatory names (`mandatoryCertHosts`), so `hosts` only adds and `removeHosts` cannot drop a canonical name. It reconciles drift only when asked and only with a gate: in direct mode `reconcileTailnet: true` moves the config and the certificate together; in caddy mode `"prepare"` mints a transition certificate and `ingress-cutover.js --tailnet-host` applies the move (see Caddy-mode tailnet move). The gate rule is `caddy.tailnetSiteGated`, which is pinned to the generator's. Tests: `test/host-inventory.test.js`.
 - **Caddy-mode tailnet move** (#1905) — `lib/tailnet-cutover.js`. The apply phase of moving the canonical tailnet host in caddy mode. `validateTailnetApply` refuses a name that is invalid, not currently observed, unchanged, ungated, or not yet in the certificate (prepare adds it). `strictHealth` is healthy only on HTTP 200 with `status: "ok"`, and for the candidate name it also requires the served certificate to carry it. `verifyTailnetApply` retries until every check is healthy in the same round. `rollbackTailnetApply` restores the Caddyfile, `caddyTailnetHost` and the reload, verifies each, and reports `rolledBack: true` only when all three are proven. `scripts/ingress-cutover.js --tailnet-host` drives it. Tests: `test/tailnet-cutover.test.js`.
 - **Per-caller project view** (#1739) — `lib/project-view.js`. Decides how much of a project row each caller of the projects API sees. The operator and the verified Project Master get every row whole, a bound project gets its own row whole, and everyone else gets an allowlisted public projection, so a field added later stays hidden by default. Tests: `test/project-view.test.js`.
+- **Wrap upstream provenance** (#1868) — `lib/wrap-steps/_upstream-provenance.js`. Says whether each uncommitted file already matches the upstream default branch, from git content alone (no GitHub call, no wrap ledger), so a session checkout left behind after its PR merged is not offered its own merged plan or an upstream-identical carrier as new work. `capture` runs in `session-files` with one bounded refresh of the default branch and records that commit; `recheck` in the commit step re-reads the tree against the same commit with no network call.
+- **Medusa exchange watchdog** (#1839) — `lib/medusa-exchanges.js`. Tracks what happened to each Medusa message after the Hub stored it, so one that never reached its reader can be seen, aged and escalated. Facts are append-only and the exchange row is a projection ranked by fact kind (`replay` recomputes it); terminal outcomes (closed, retracted, undeliverable, recipient_retired) are decided inside one `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` transaction so exactly one wins.
+- **Medusa tracked send** (#1839) — `lib/medusa-send.js`. The one path every switchboard send takes: validate delivery metadata against what the caller can prove, record the intent after TangleClaw's own checks, then bind the Hub's id or record what the Hub answered (refused as undeliverable, lost as `send_unknown`, never re-sent). An exchange whose Hub answer is known but cannot be bound is still reported as sent, so a caller is not invited to send a second copy.
 
